# A type lambda is a TYPE, and Tier 0 discards types — SSC3-7i

`type Pair = [A] =>> (A, A)` and `val p: Pair[Long] = …` both vanish, leaving an ordinary tuple.
That is why this needed no type checker and no generics: it was behind the wall only in the
sprint's guess, and the UniML front had accepted it all along.

The numbers are chosen so the expectation can be read: `20 % 7` is 6, `20 % 11` is 9, and 6 + 9
is **15**. The second alias is applied twice over to show that a type lambda used in another
type is skipped the same way.

```scalascript
type Pair = [A] =>> (A, A)
type Boxed = [A] =>> Pair[A]

def combine(p: Pair[Long]): Long = p._1 + p._2

val n: Long = 20
val p: Pair[Long] = (n % 7, n % 11)
println(combine(p))

val q: Boxed[Long] = (1, 2)
println(q._1 + q._2)
```
